{"kind":"artifact","text":"internal/statusview at exact clean pushed head 3494ea3c9330d6a1d273cab92237572c152cde92, branch request/bounded-status-selection. Fulfils request 1c1075e1 under promise 304b3264.\n\nWHAT CHANGED. Build selected rows after shaping them; it now selects before. The first pass still walks the whole projection, because the totals and the omitted counts are facts about the whole log and would be wrong computed from a sample, but it keeps only source records, which are already resident and copy no text. Cap runs on those. Only the survivors go through Text and ActorName. artifactState is extracted so the totals pass and the shaping pass cannot disagree about what \"current\" means.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS BEYOND SPEED. The text being shaped is attacker-influenced, and request 099e4c83 will make that shaping heavier by escaping terminal control sequences rather than merely collapsing whitespace. Bounding the work first means that cost lands on twenty rows rather than on every row in the log. That is why I took these two in this order.\n\nTHE CLAIM AND HOW IT IS PROVEN. The claim is that output is unchanged. \"The tests still pass\" does not show that: those tests were written against the old shape and would survive a wrong reordering. So I captured 27,256 bytes from this file's previous implementation at main b7f5b015, before the split, and the committed test asserts the new code reproduces them exactly. The golden comes from the old code, not from the new code certifying itself.\n\nThe fixture is built to be hostile to this specific change. Every list is populated -- both commitment lanes, current, stale and retired artifacts, ineffective and disputed attempts, and dissents with provenance -- so no lane is silently unexercised. All three artifact note flags vary on different cycles, because artifactState is the part most able to drift from the switch it replaced. Terminal control sequences and a bidi override sit in every user-controlled field, so when that text is escaped rather than trimmed, this test says whether the escaping moved with it.\n\nMUTATION EVIDENCE, against the committed test rather than a scratch harness. Selecting the oldest rows instead of the newest: caught. Counting totals from the kept sample instead of the whole projection: caught. Dropping one note from artifactState: caught. Green here is load-bearing.\n\nMEASURED, not asserted. Same benchmark run in a b7f5b015 worktree and in this one, 5000-row projection with hostile text: 16,131,567 ns/op, 41,650,115 B/op and 65,105 allocs/op became 2,012,083 ns/op, 10,111,939 B/op and 373 allocs/op. Eight times faster, four times less memory, 175 times fewer allocations. Sixty-five thousand allocations to render twenty rows was the defect; the allocation count is the number that names it.\n\nWHAT I CHECKED BEFORE MOVING ANYTHING. Selecting before shaping is sound only if selection does not depend on a shaped value. It does not: lane membership comes from the commitment status, the artifact Retired and Stale flags, the decision verdict and the statement kind; ordering is projection order throughout, then Cap keeps the tail and reverse flips it. I would have reported the opposite rather than quietly reordering.\n\nSCOPE. One file plus its test and testdata. No caching. No change to ListCap, TextCap or DeltaCap. No public signature changed. docs/reference/architecture.md is untouched because no layer contract moves: this is the same output produced with less work.\n\nGATES at the exact head, all run: gofmt clean; go vet ./... clean; go test -count=1 ./internal/statusview 0.519s; go test -count=1 ./... with no failing package; make docs ok 60.479s; git diff --check clean. Worktree clean, based on current main b7f5b015, pushed.","body":{"branch":"request/bounded-status-selection","commit":"3494ea3c9330d6a1d273cab92237572c152cde92","path":"internal/statusview","tests":"gofmt; go vet ./...; go test -count=1 ./internal/statusview; go test -count=1 ./...; make docs; git diff --check; cross-tree byte equivalence against b7f5b015; allocation benchmark old vs new"}}